window.EN = window.EN || {};
window.EN.lexikon = (window.EN.lexikon || []).concat([
  { key: "Logotherapie", begriff: "Logotherapy", kurz: "Meaning-centered psychotherapy founded by Viktor E. Frankl, which understands the human being as a spiritually free and responsible being oriented toward meaning.", text: "Logotherapy was developed by the Viennese neurologist and psychiatrist Viktor E. Frankl from the late 1920s onward and was systematically elaborated after the Second World War. The name derives from the Greek “logos,” which Frankl understood in the sense of “meaning.” In the history of Viennese psychotherapy it is often referred to – after psychoanalysis and individual psychology – as the “Third Viennese School of Psychotherapy.”\n\nAt its center are three anthropological core assumptions: the freedom of will, the will to meaning, and the meaning of life. The human being is understood not merely as a psychophysical but as a spiritual being who can take a stand toward his or her conditions. The theory of illness and the therapy therefore take into account noetic (spiritual) factors in addition to somatic and psychological ones.\n\nIts specific methods include paradoxical intention, dereflection, modification of attitudes, and the Socratic dialogue. Logotherapy does not see itself as a replacement for but as a complement to other psychotherapeutic approaches; its actual field of indication comprises noogenic neuroses, crises of meaning, and coping with unavoidable suffering." },
  { key: "Existenzanalyse", begriff: "Existential Analysis", kurz: "Term for the anthropological and theoretical foundation of logotherapy in Frankl's work and for the independent psychotherapeutic approach further developed by Alfred Längle.", text: "In Viktor E. Frankl's work, “existential analysis” (Existenzanalyse) denotes the philosophical-anthropological analysis of human existence with regard to its responsibleness and its relatedness to meaning. For Frankl, existential analysis and logotherapy relate to each other as theory and practice: existential analysis provides the image of the human being, logotherapy the treatment method derived from it.\n\nFrom the 1980s onward, Alfred Längle and colleagues of the Society for Logotherapy and Existential Analysis (GLE) expanded existential analysis into a comprehensive, phenomenological-personal psychotherapeutic approach. Central to it are the four fundamental motivations of existence and Personal Existential Analysis as a process model. The focus thereby shifts from the question of meaning alone to the emotional consent to one's own life and to the capacity to act with inner consent.\n\nIn Austria, existential analysis (like logotherapy) is a recognized psychotherapeutic method under the Psychotherapy Act (Psychotherapiegesetz). In professional usage, the terms should be specified according to the respective reference author." },
  { key: "Wille zum Sinn", begriff: "Will to Meaning", kurz: "According to Frankl, the primary motivation of the human being to find and fulfill meaning in life.", text: "Frankl deliberately contrasted the will to meaning with the “will to pleasure” (Freud) and the “will to power” (Adler). In his view, pleasure and power are not primary goals but side effects or means: pleasure arises when a meaning is fulfilled, and it is missed precisely when it is directly intended.\n\nThe will to meaning is not to be understood as a drive in the psychodynamic sense. The human being is not “driven” by meaning but “drawn” by values and freely decides to respond to them. If the will to meaning remains unfulfilled, Frankl speaks of existential frustration, which can lead to noogenic neurosis." },
  { key: "Freiheit des Willens", begriff: "Freedom of Will", kurz: "First anthropological core assumption of logotherapy: the human being is conditioned but not determined and can take a stand toward his or her conditions.", text: "Logotherapy does not deny that human beings are subject to biological, psychological, and sociological conditions. It emphasizes, however, that they are not completely at the mercy of these conditions: freedom is understood as freedom “to” take a stand toward the given circumstances, not as freedom “from” all conditions.\n\nFor Frankl, freedom is inseparably linked with responsibility. In contrast to deterministic images of the human being, which he criticized as pandeterminism, he regards freedom of will as the precondition of any meaning-oriented psychotherapy." },
  { key: "Sinn des Lebens", begriff: "Meaning of Life", kurz: "Third anthropological core assumption of logotherapy: life has meaning under all circumstances, a meaning that is found, not invented, in the concrete situation.", text: "Frankl speaks less of a general meaning of life than of the concrete “meaning of the moment,” which presents itself uniquely to a particular person in a particular situation. According to this view, meaning cannot be arbitrarily given but only discovered; conscience is regarded as the organ by which the human being senses it.\n\nFrankl describes three categories of values as paths to the realization of meaning: creative values, experiential values, and attitudinal values. Because attitudinal values can be realized even in the face of unavoidable suffering, logotherapy maintains that life can remain meaningful up to one's last breath.\n\nFrankl distinguishes this situational meaning from “supra-meaning,” an all-encompassing meaning that exceeds human comprehension and is an object of faith and trust, not of knowledge." },
  { key: "Kopernikanische Wende", begriff: "Copernican Turn", kurz: "Frankl's reversal of the question of meaning: it is not the human being who should ask life about its meaning; rather, he or she is the one being questioned by life.", text: "With this image Frankl described a fundamental change of perspective: instead of asking what one can still expect from life, the human being should recognize that life expects something from him or her. One answers this question not by talking but by responsible action and attitude.\n\nThe idea is already found in *Ärztliche Seelsorge* (The Doctor and the Soul) and is illustrated in Frankl's account of his experiences in the concentration camp through conversations with fellow prisoners. Therapeutically, the reversal serves to transform a passive, demanding attitude toward life into an active, responsive one." },
  { key: "Übersinn", begriff: "Supra-Meaning", kurz: "In Frankl's work, the comprehensive, ultimate meaning of the whole, which exceeds the human capacity for knowledge.", text: "Frankl distinguishes between the concrete meaning of a situation, which is in principle accessible to the human being, and a supra-meaning, which eludes rational knowledge. The human being cannot approach supra-meaning by knowing, but only by believing or trusting.\n\nLogotherapy nonetheless remains open with regard to worldview: it describes the possibility of such a comprehensive meaning anthropologically but does not prescribe any religious interpretation. Frankl expressly emphasized that medical ministry (ärztliche Seelsorge) does not seek to replace pastoral care in the religious sense." },
  { key: "Noogene Neurose", begriff: "Noogenic Neurosis", kurz: "Neurotic disorder which, according to Frankl, arises not primarily from psychological conflicts but from spiritual problems, conflicts of values, or existential frustration.", text: "Frankl distinguished somatogenic, psychogenic, and noogenic neuroses according to their main cause. In noogenic neurosis the root lies in the noetic dimension, for example in a conflict of conscience, a collision of values, or an unfulfilled will to meaning; the symptoms themselves, however, manifest in the psychological domain.\n\nFrankl regarded logotherapy as the specific therapy for noogenic neurosis. The term is a logotherapeutic category and not a diagnosis in current classification systems; clinically relevant disorders are to be diagnosed according to ICD-11 or DSM-5-TR. Empirical approaches have been made, among others, with the Purpose in Life Test." },
  { key: "Existenzielles Vakuum", begriff: "Existential Vacuum", kurz: "Experience of inner emptiness and meaninglessness described by Frankl, which he regarded as a widespread phenomenon of modern societies.", text: "Frankl traced the existential vacuum back to a twofold loss: unlike animals, human beings are no longer told by instincts what they must do, and – as a result of the decline of traditions – no longer told by traditions what they ought to do. This gives rise to the danger of either doing what others do (conformism) or doing what others want (totalitarianism).\n\nThe existential vacuum is not pathological in itself. It frequently manifests as boredom and indifference and can be masked by substitute gratifications such as striving for power, consumption, or addictive behavior. Only when symptoms develop from it does Frankl speak of a noogenic neurosis." },
  { key: "Existenzielle Frustration", begriff: "Existential Frustration", kurz: "State in which the will to meaning remains unfulfilled.", text: "By existential frustration Frankl means the frustration of the will to meaning. “Existential” can refer to existence itself, to the meaning of existence, or to the striving for meaning.\n\nAccording to the logotherapeutic view, existential frustration is neither morbid nor pathological per se but an expression of a specifically human questioning attitude. It requires treatment only when it turns into a noogenic neurosis. Elisabeth Lukas made it accessible to measurement with the Logo-Test." },
  { key: "Noetische Dimension", begriff: "Noetic Dimension", kurz: "The specifically human, spiritual dimension in which freedom, responsibility, conscience, and orientation toward meaning are located.", text: "Frankl used “noetic” (from Greek “nous,” mind/spirit) synonymously with “spiritual” (geistig) in order to avoid confusion with religious connotations. “Noological,” by contrast, denotes the study of the spiritual dimension. The noetic dimension includes, among other things, the capacities for self-distancing and self-transcendence.\n\nAccording to Frankl, the spiritual person can be hindered in its expression by illness but cannot itself become ill. From this, logotherapy derives a therapeutic optimism: even in severe mental illness, a spiritual core remains addressable, to which treatment can appeal." },
  { key: "Dimensionalontologie", begriff: "Dimensional Ontology", kurz: "Frankl's model of the human being as a unity in spite of multiplicity in the somatic, psychological, and noetic dimensions.", text: "Frankl opposed layer models that compose the human being of separate levels. Instead, he described body, psyche, and spirit as dimensions of a unified whole, each of which the various sciences capture only in a projection.\n\nHe illustrated this geometrically in two “laws”: one and the same thing, projected out of its own dimension into different lower dimensions, yields contradictory images; different things, projected into the same lower dimension, yield ambiguous images. This implies a critique of reductionism: the findings of an individual science are not false but incomplete when they are taken for the whole." },
  { key: "Trotzmacht des Geistes", begriff: "Defiant Power of the Human Spirit", kurz: "The human capacity to stand up, by virtue of the spiritual dimension, against inner and outer conditions.", text: "By the defiant power of the human spirit Frankl means the possibility of adopting one's own stance toward psychological states, moods, fears, or unfavorable life circumstances. According to this view, a person does not have to put up with everything from himself or herself.\n\nThe defiant power is closely connected with self-distancing and forms a basis of paradoxical intention and the modification of attitudes. It should not be misunderstood as mere effort of will but as a spiritual stance that can also include humor and composure." },
  { key: "Selbstdistanzierung", begriff: "Self-Distancing", kurz: "The specifically human capacity to gain distance from oneself, one's own feelings, thoughts, and symptoms.", text: "Alongside self-transcendence, self-distancing is one of the two fundamental capacities of the noetic dimension. It makes it possible to view oneself, as it were, from the outside and not to identify with every psychological impulse.\n\nTherapeutically, self-distancing is used above all in paradoxical intention, in which humor acts as a special form of taking distance. There are structural parallels to concepts such as cognitive defusion in Acceptance and Commitment Therapy or the decentered stance in mindfulness-based approaches, although the theoretical foundations differ." },
  { key: "Selbsttranszendenz", begriff: "Self-Transcendence", kurz: "The orientation of the human being beyond himself or herself toward something or someone other than oneself.", text: "According to Frankl, human existence is essentially self-transcendent: it always points beyond itself to a task, a cause, or another person. Self-actualization therefore cannot be attained as a direct goal but arises as a side effect of devotion to a meaning.\n\nThis view grounds logotherapy's critique of excessive self-centeredness and forms the anthropological basis of dereflection. Frankl compared the human being to the eye, which sees well precisely when it does not see itself." },
  { key: "Paradoxe Intention", begriff: "Paradoxical Intention", kurz: "Logotherapeutic technique in which patients are guided to deliberately wish for or do the very thing they fear.", text: "Paradoxical intention targets the vicious circle of symptom, anticipatory anxiety, and symptom intensification that can occur in phobias, obsessive-compulsive phenomena, and sleep and sexual dysfunctions. Instead of fleeing from or fighting the symptom, the patient intentionally wishes for it – often in a humorously exaggerated way. This removes the basis of the anxiety.\n\nAccording to Frankl, the mechanism of action is self-distancing. Frankl first described the method in a publication in 1939 and presented it to an English-speaking professional audience in 1960. The technique overlaps with behavioral procedures such as exposure and with paradoxical interventions of systemic therapy. It is considered contraindicated in depression with suicidality and in psychoses." },
  { key: "Dereflexion", begriff: "Dereflection", kurz: "Logotherapeutic method that reduces excessive self-observation (hyperreflection) by directing attention to meaningful content outside one's own person.", text: "Dereflection is based on the capacity for self-transcendence. It is used particularly where anxious observation of a process – for instance falling asleep or sexuality – disturbs that very process.\n\nAccording to Frankl, merely ignoring a symptom is not enough; what is decisive is the positive turning toward something meaningful, which renders the symptom “superfluous,” as it were. Paradoxical intention and dereflection complement each other: the one counters hyperintention, the other hyperreflection." },
  { key: "Hyperreflexion", begriff: "Hyperreflection", kurz: "Excessive, often anxious self-observation that disturbs natural processes.", text: "By hyperreflection Frankl means a maladaptive attitude in which a person makes himself or herself the object of constant observation. Spontaneous processes such as sleep, concentration, speaking, or sexual experience are impaired as a result.\n\nHyperreflection frequently interacts with hyperintention. Its logotherapeutic remedy is dereflection. Related constructs in more recent research include self-focused attention and rumination." },
  { key: "Hyperintention", begriff: "Hyperintention", kurz: "Excessive, forced striving for a goal that prevents its very attainment.", text: "Frankl observed that certain states – pleasure, happiness, sleep, relaxation – arise only as effects and are missed when they are pursued directly. In his formulation, “happiness” is not a goal but a consequence of the fulfillment of meaning.\n\nHyperintention is closely linked with hyperreflection and can be intensified by anticipatory anxiety. Paradoxical intention reverses this dynamic by intending the opposite of what is desired." },
  { key: "Erwartungsangst", begriff: "Anticipatory Anxiety", kurz: "Fear of the recurrence of a symptom that promotes that very symptom.", text: "Frankl described anticipatory anxiety as the central mechanism of neurotic vicious circles: a symptom generates the fear of its recurrence, the fear intensifies the symptom, and the symptom in turn confirms the fear. Typical response patterns are flight from fear (in phobias), fight against compulsion (in obsessive-compulsive phenomena), and fight for pleasure (in sexual dysfunctions).\n\nThe concept shows clear parallels to the construct of “fear of fear” in the cognitive-behavioral model of panic disorder. In logotherapy, anticipatory anxiety is treated primarily with paradoxical intention." },
  { key: "Einstellungsmodulation", begriff: "Modification of Attitudes", kurz: "Logotherapeutic method for changing pathogenic or meaning-obstructing attitudes toward healthier, meaning-oriented stances.", text: "Modification of attitudes addresses the stance with which a person meets his or her situation. Where external circumstances cannot be changed, the attitude toward them can be changed; this makes the realization of attitudinal values possible.\n\nThe method was systematized for practice above all by Elisabeth Lukas. Methodologically, it frequently makes use of the Socratic dialogue. In terms of content, there are points of contact with cognitive restructuring, although modification of attitudes is oriented more toward value references and possibilities of meaning than toward examining whether cognitions are realistic." },
  { key: "Sokratischer Dialog", begriff: "Socratic Dialogue", kurz: "Questioning style of conversation that helps clients discover their own insights, values, and possibilities of meaning for themselves.", text: "Following Socrates' “art of midwifery” (maieutics), the Socratic dialogue dispenses with instruction and advice. Instead, targeted questions bring contradictions, hidden resources, and value references to light.\n\nIn logotherapy, the Socratic dialogue serves primarily the discovery of meaning and the modification of attitudes; meaning is not to be prescribed by the therapist but found by the person himself or herself. In Beck's cognitive therapy, “Socratic questioning” is also a central technique, there with an emphasis on testing dysfunctional assumptions." },
  { key: "Schöpferische Werte", begriff: "Creative Values", kurz: "First of Frankl's three categories of values: realization of meaning through doing, creating, and shaping.", text: "Creative values are realized when a person gives something to the world – through work, a creation, a deed, or a contribution to others. What is decisive is not the scope or social significance of an activity but the manner in which it is carried out.\n\nFrankl emphasized that the loss of work does not automatically mean a loss of meaning; in connection with “unemployment neurosis” he described the equation of employment with the meaning of life as a maladaptive attitude." },
  { key: "Erlebniswerte", begriff: "Experiential Values", kurz: "Frankl's second category of values: realization of meaning through receiving and experiencing what is valuable, for instance in nature, art, and love.", text: "Experiential values are realized when a person takes something in from the world: in the encounter with another human being, in love, in the enjoyment of art, or in the experience of nature. Frankl stressed that even a single moment of intense experience can make a life meaningful.\n\nLove occupies a special position in this category because, according to Frankl, it grasps the other person in his or her uniqueness and makes his or her potential values visible." },
  { key: "Einstellungswerte", begriff: "Attitudinal Values", kurz: "Third and, according to Frankl, highest category of values: realization of meaning through the stance a person takes toward unavoidable suffering.", text: "Where neither creative nor experiential values are accessible – for example in incurable illness, unavoidable loss, or guilt – there remains, according to Frankl, the possibility of realizing meaning through the way one bears one's fate. He expressly emphasized, however, that avoidable suffering is to be eliminated; only necessary, fateful suffering opens up the possibility of attitudinal values.\n\nAttitudinal values are the foundation of the logotherapeutic claim that life can remain meaningful under all conditions. They are of particular importance in palliative care, psycho-oncology, and grief counseling." },
  { key: "Tragische Trias", begriff: "Tragic Triad", kurz: "Frankl's term for the three inescapable basic facts of human existence: suffering, guilt, and death.", text: "No human being can escape the experience of suffering, the possibility of guilt, and the certainty of death. Logotherapy therefore does not ask how these facts can be avoided but how they can be shaped meaningfully.\n\nAccording to Frankl, suffering can be turned into achievement, guilt can be taken as an occasion to change for the better, and transitoriness can be understood as an incentive to responsible action. The concept of tragic optimism refers to this stance." },
  { key: "Tragischer Optimismus", begriff: "Tragic Optimism", kurz: "The stance of remaining optimistic in the face of the tragic triad of suffering, guilt, and death and making the best of every situation.", text: "Frankl developed the concept in a postscript added to later editions of his book *Man's Search for Meaning* from 1984 onward. Tragic optimism does not mean glossing over suffering but affirming the potential to adopt a meaningful stance even under the most adverse circumstances.\n\nThe term has been taken up in more recent positive and existential psychology, among others by Paul T. P. Wong, who understands it as a component of a mature well-being that integrates suffering." },
  { key: "Homo patiens", begriff: "Homo patiens", kurz: "Frankl's image of the suffering human being who can realize meaning through the way he or she suffers.", text: "Frankl contrasted “homo faber,” who realizes meaning through success and achievement, with “homo patiens,” who finds meaning in bearing suffering upright. According to Frankl, the two axes – success and failure on the one hand, fulfillment and despair on the other – are orthogonal to each other: a person can fail outwardly and nevertheless find fulfillment.\n\nFrankl developed these ideas in his work *Homo patiens. Versuch einer Pathodizee* (Homo Patiens: An Attempt at a Pathodicy, 1950)." },
  { key: "Sinnfindung", begriff: "Discovery of Meaning", kurz: "Process in which a person perceives the possibilities of meaning inherent in a situation, evaluates them, and decides to realize them.", text: "According to the logotherapeutic understanding, the discovery of meaning comprises several steps: perceiving the situation, perceiving the possibilities it contains, evaluating them in the light of values and conscience, and deciding and acting. Meaning is understood as something that is found, not produced.\n\nEmpirical research on meaning distinguishes between experiencing existing meaning (“presence of meaning”) and searching for meaning (“search for meaning”). The discovery of meaning is a central goal of meaning-centered counseling and therapy." },
  { key: "Gewissen als Sinnorgan", begriff: "Conscience as the Organ of Meaning", kurz: "Logotherapeutic conception of conscience as the intuitive capacity to sense the unique meaning of a concrete situation.", text: "Frankl understood conscience not primarily as an internalized normative authority in the sense of the psychoanalytic superego but as an intuitive organ oriented toward the unique meaning inherent in the situation. Because situations are unique, general norms cannot replace conscience.\n\nAt the same time, Frankl emphasized the fallibility of conscience: a person can never be ultimately certain of having grasped the meaning. From this follows an attitude of tolerance toward the conscience-based decisions of others." },
  { key: "Verantwortlichkeit", begriff: "Responsibleness", kurz: "According to Frankl, the essence of human existence: the human being is responsible for realizing the meaning assigned to him or her in his or her life situation.", text: "In logotherapy, freedom and responsibleness form an inseparable pair. Frankl defined existential analysis as the analysis of human existence with regard to its responsibleness and regarded it as the goal of therapy to lead people to an awareness of their responsibleness.\n\nLogotherapy leaves open for what and to whom a person feels responsible – to society, to one's own conscience, or to God. This openness is an expression of its neutrality with regard to worldview." },
  { key: "Sonntagsneurose", begriff: "Sunday Neurosis", kurz: "Depressive mood described by Frankl that occurs when, with the absence of everyday occupation, inner emptiness becomes palpable.", text: "During the week, the experience of meaninglessness can be covered up by work and routine. On weekends, on vacation, or after retirement, by contrast, the existential vacuum emerges more clearly.\n\nSunday neurosis is not a diagnostic term but a phenomenological description of a widespread experience. It illustrates that occupation alone does not yet amount to fulfillment of meaning." },
  { key: "Kollektive Neurose", begriff: "Collective Neurosis", kurz: "Frankl's term of cultural diagnosis for widespread maladaptive attitudes, which he understood as an expression of a spiritual crisis of the present age.", text: "Frankl described four symptoms of the “collective neurosis”: the provisional attitude toward existence (living from day to day), the fatalistic attitude toward life, collectivist thinking, and fanaticism. According to Frankl, what they all have in common is the flight from freedom and responsibility.\n\nThe term is explicitly meant as cultural critique and not as a clinical diagnosis. Frankl developed it in his work *Pathologie des Zeitgeistes* (Pathology of the Zeitgeist, 1955)." },
  { key: "Pandeterminismus", begriff: "Pandeterminism", kurz: "View criticized by Frankl that the human being is completely determined by biological, psychological, or social conditions.", text: "Frankl distinguished pandeterminism from a legitimate determinism: he did not dispute that the human being is conditioned; he did, however, oppose the assumption that human behavior can be derived entirely from these conditions. Such an image of the human being, he argued, deprives the person of his or her responsibility.\n\nThe critique of pandeterminism is closely connected with the critique of reductionism and with the concept of freedom of will." },
  { key: "Reduktionismus", begriff: "Reductionism", kurz: "Explanatory approach that reduces the human being to one of his or her dimensions – for instance the biological or the psychological; criticized in logotherapy as “nothing-but thinking.”", text: "Frankl criticized statements following the pattern “The human being is nothing but …” as an illegitimate generalization of the findings of individual sciences. Such reductionism, he argued, overlooks the noetic dimension and thus freedom, responsibility, and orientation toward meaning.\n\nIn his dimensional ontology, Frankl explains why different sciences provide legitimate but in each case only partial projections of the human being. Methodological reductionism in research is not rejected thereby, as long as it is not elevated to a worldview statement about the essence of the human being." },
  { key: "Ärztliche Seelsorge", begriff: "Medical Ministry", kurz: "Title of Frankl's principal work (1946) and term for the medical-psychotherapeutic accompaniment of people in spiritual and existential distress.", text: "*Ärztliche Seelsorge. Grundlagen der Logotherapie und Existenzanalyse* (published in English as The Doctor and the Soul) is regarded as Frankl's foundational work. A first manuscript was lost during his deportation; he reconstructed it after his liberation, and it appeared in Vienna in 1946.\n\nBy “medical ministry” (ärztliche Seelsorge) Frankl meant the physician's task of also taking a position on patients' worldview and existential questions, for example in the case of incurable illness. He expressly distinguished it from religious pastoral care, which it was not meant to replace." },
  { key: "Höhenpsychologie", begriff: "Height Psychology", kurz: "Frankl's programmatic term for a psychology that takes into account not only the “depths” but also the spiritual “heights” of the human being.", text: "Frankl coined the term (Höhenpsychologie) in the 1930s as a complement to depth psychology. While the latter investigates instinctual and unconscious forces, height psychology focuses on the striving for meaning, values, and responsibility.\n\nThe term is not to be understood as a rejection of depth-psychological insights but as a call for an anthropological expansion. It illustrates logotherapy's claim to supplement psychotherapy with the noetic dimension." },
  { key: "Purpose-in-Life-Test", begriff: "Purpose in Life Test", kurz: "Questionnaire (PIL) developed by James C. Crumbaugh and Leonard T. Maholick to assess experienced meaning in life.", text: "The Purpose in Life Test was developed in the 1960s to make Frankl's concepts of the existential vacuum and noogenic neurosis empirically accessible. The quantitative Part A comprises 20 items with seven-point response scales; it is supplemented by a sentence-completion part (B) and a free-text part (C).\n\nThe PIL is one of the most frequently used instruments in meaning research. Its factor structure and its overlap with measures of depressiveness and well-being, among other things, have been critically discussed, raising questions of construct validity. Newer instruments, such as the Meaning in Life Questionnaire (Steger et al.), additionally distinguish between experienced meaning and the search for meaning." },
  { key: "Logo-Test", begriff: "Logo-Test", kurz: "German-language instrument developed by Elisabeth Lukas to assess inner fulfillment of meaning and existential frustration.", text: "The Logo-Test was developed by Elisabeth Lukas building on Frankl's concept of existential frustration and was published as a test instrument. It is divided into several parts, which include both self-reports on life satisfaction and fulfillment of meaning and appraisals of life situations.\n\nIn German-speaking countries, the Logo-Test has been used primarily in logotherapeutic research and practice. As with all self-report instruments, psychometric quality criteria and norms must be taken into account in interpretation." },
  { key: "Existenzielle Grundmotivationen", begriff: "Fundamental Existential Motivations", kurz: "The four basic conditions of fulfilled existence described by Alfred Längle: being able to be, liking to live, being allowed to be oneself, and wanting to do something meaningful.", text: "Längle assigns human existence four fundamental motivations, each of which answers a basic question. The first concerns being able to be in the world (support, space, protection), the second liking to live (relationship, time, closeness), the third being allowed to be oneself (attention, justice, appreciation), and the fourth meaningful action in a larger context (field of activity, structural context, value in the future).\n\nIn existential analysis, the model serves as a structural model for diagnosis and therapy. Mental disorders are assigned to the fundamental motivations; anxiety disorders, for example, are associated primarily with the first and depressive disorders with the second fundamental motivation." },
  { key: "Personale Existenzanalyse", begriff: "Personal Existential Analysis", kurz: "Phenomenological process model (PEA) developed by Alfred Längle that structures the processing of experiences up to coherent action.", text: "Personal Existential Analysis describes how a person takes in impressions, takes a stand toward them, and translates them into authentic action. A description of the facts is followed by the phenomenological analysis of the impression (feeling, impulse, content), the inner taking of a stand, and finally expression in the form of a response or action.\n\nPEA is a central methodological element of existential analysis according to Längle and is used in psychotherapy, counseling, and coaching. It combines elements of phenomenological philosophy with psychotherapeutic process work." },
  { key: "Meaning-Centered Psychotherapy", begriff: "Meaning-Centered Psychotherapy", kurz: "Manualized, meaning-centered psychotherapy for people with advanced cancer, developed by William Breitbart and colleagues at Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center.", text: "Meaning-Centered Psychotherapy (MCP) translates Frankl's basic ideas into a structured, time-limited treatment format. It exists as a group format (Meaning-Centered Group Psychotherapy) and as an individual format (Individual Meaning-Centered Psychotherapy). Topics include sources of meaning, one's own life story as a source of meaning, limitations and responsibility, as well as experiential, creative, and attitudinal values.\n\nMCP has been studied in randomized controlled trials and is regarded as one of the best-evaluated meaning-centered approaches in psycho-oncology. Adaptations for further target groups, such as family caregivers, have been developed." },
  { key: "Meaning-Centered Counseling", begriff: "Meaning-Centered Counseling", kurz: "Integrative counseling and therapy approach developed by Paul T. P. Wong that combines logotherapy with cognitive-behavioral and positive-psychological elements.", text: "Meaning-Centered Counseling and Therapy (MCCT) understands meaning as a central resource for coping and well-being. Wong integrates Frankl's image of the human being into a pragmatic, psychoeducationally oriented approach that works, among other things, with the PURE model (Purpose, Understanding, Responsible action, Enjoyment/Evaluation).\n\nWong is also a proponent of a “second wave” of positive psychology, which explicitly incorporates negative experiences and suffering into the understanding of a flourishing life. Here he builds on Frankl's concept of tragic optimism." },
  { key: "Sinnkrise", begriff: "Crisis of Meaning", kurz: "Phase of life in which previous sources of meaning become questionable or are lost and the question of the purpose of one's own life becomes pressing.", text: "Crises of meaning can be triggered by critical life events such as loss, illness, separation, job loss, or transitions such as retirement, but they can also develop gradually. They are frequently accompanied by emptiness, disorientation, and reduced drive.\n\nA crisis of meaning is not per se a mental disorder; it can also be the starting point of a reorientation. In terms of differential diagnosis, however, a depressive illness in particular must be ruled out, since symptoms can overlap. Meaning-centered counseling supports the recovery or rediscovery of sources of meaning." },
  { key: "Existenzangst", begriff: "Existential Anxiety", kurz: "Anxiety relating to the basic conditions of existence such as finitude, uncertainty, freedom, or meaninglessness; in colloquial German (Existenzangst) also fear for one's material livelihood.", text: "In the tradition of existential philosophy, for instance in Kierkegaard and Heidegger, anxiety is distinguished from fear: fear is directed at a specific object, anxiety at existence as a whole. Rollo May and Irvin D. Yalom carried this distinction over into psychotherapy and regarded “normal” existential anxiety as an unavoidable part of human life.\n\nIn existential analysis according to Längle, anxiety is associated primarily with the first fundamental motivation, support in existence. In everyday German usage, by contrast, “Existenzangst” usually refers to worry about work, income, and material security; the two meanings should be distinguished in professional usage." },
  { key: "Wertimagination", begriff: "Value Imagination", kurz: "Imaginative method developed by Uwe Böschemeyer that combines logotherapy with work on inner images in order to make values and resources tangible.", text: "Value imagination (Wertimagination) builds on Frankl's theory of values and supplements it with imaginative approaches. In guided inner imagery journeys, the person is meant to gain access not only to distressing but above all to valuable, strengthening content.\n\nThe method is used predominantly in counseling, personal development, and psychotherapy in German-speaking countries. Systematic efficacy studies are so far available only to a limited extent." },
  { key: "Existenzielle Psychotherapie", begriff: "Existential Psychotherapy", kurz: "Umbrella term for psychotherapeutic approaches that start from the basic givens of human existence; in the narrower sense, the approach of Irvin D. Yalom.", text: "In his textbook *Existential Psychotherapy* (1980), Yalom described four “ultimate concerns” with which every human being is confronted: death, freedom, existential isolation, and meaninglessness. Psychological symptoms are understood as forms of defense against the anxiety that arises from confrontation with these givens.\n\nIn the broader sense, the existential tradition includes, besides Yalom, Rollo May, the Daseinsanalysis of Binswanger and Boss, and logotherapy and existential analysis. What they have in common is recourse to existential philosophy and an emphasis on freedom, responsibility, and encounter in the therapeutic relationship." },
  { key: "Daseinsanalyse", begriff: "Daseinsanalysis", kurz: "School of psychiatry and psychotherapy based on Martin Heidegger's analytic of Dasein, founded by Ludwig Binswanger and further developed by Medard Boss.", text: "Ludwig Binswanger applied Heidegger's analysis of “being-in-the-world” to psychiatry and attempted to understand mental illnesses as modifications of the way in which a person projects and experiences his or her world. In close exchange with Heidegger – documented in the *Zollikoner Seminare* (Zollikon Seminars) – Medard Boss developed from this an independent daseinsanalytic psychotherapy.\n\nDaseinsanalysis is to be distinguished from Frankl's existential analysis, even though the two terms were historically used partly interchangeably. It largely dispenses with theoretical constructs and relies on phenomenological understanding of what shows itself in experience." },
  { key: "Viktor E. Frankl", begriff: "Viktor E. Frankl", kurz: "Austrian neurologist and psychiatrist (1905–1997), founder of logotherapy and existential analysis.", text: "Viktor Emil Frankl was born in Vienna on March 26, 1905, and died there on September 2, 1997. He studied medicine at the University of Vienna, was in contact with Sigmund Freud in his youth, and for a time belonged to the circle around Alfred Adler, from whom he parted at the end of the 1920s. During this period he organized youth counseling centers in Vienna.\n\nIn 1942 Frankl was deported with his family to the Theresienstadt ghetto; he was later imprisoned in Auschwitz and in subcamps of the Dachau concentration camp. His parents, his brother, and his first wife perished in the camps. After his liberation in 1945 he published *Ärztliche Seelsorge* (The Doctor and the Soul) and his account of his experiences in the concentration camp, which gained worldwide circulation in English translation as *Man's Search for Meaning*.\n\nFrom 1946 to 1971 Frankl headed the Vienna Neurological Polyclinic. He was professor of neurology and psychiatry at the University of Vienna and taught as a visiting professor in the United States, among other places. His work comprises numerous books on logotherapy and existential analysis." },
  { key: "Alfred Längle", begriff: "Alfred Längle", kurz: "Austrian physician, psychiatrist, and psychotherapist (born 1951), founder of modern, personal-existential existential analysis.", text: "Alfred Längle was a student and collaborator of Viktor E. Frankl and, together with him and other colleagues, founded the Society for Logotherapy and Existential Analysis (GLE) in Vienna in 1983. From the late 1980s onward he developed existential analysis further into an independent psychotherapeutic approach, which led to a separation from Frankl.\n\nLängle's central contributions are the model of the four fundamental motivations of existence and Personal Existential Analysis (PEA) as a process model. He has taught at various universities in Austria and abroad and has disseminated existential analysis internationally, particularly in Eastern Europe and Latin America." },
  { key: "Elisabeth Lukas", begriff: "Elisabeth Lukas", kurz: "Austrian psychologist and psychotherapist (born 1942), student of Frankl and one of the most influential representatives of logotherapy.", text: "Elisabeth Lukas studied psychology in Vienna and was trained in logotherapy by Viktor E. Frankl; she developed the Logo-Test as an instrument of logotherapeutic research. For many years she directed the South German Institute for Logotherapy (Süddeutsches Institut für Logotherapie) in Fürstenfeldbruck near Munich.\n\nLukas systematized logotherapy particularly for the practice of counseling and psychotherapy, for example the method of modification of attitudes. Her extensive writings include professional books as well as accessible presentations of logotherapeutic guidance for living." },
  { key: "Uwe Böschemeyer", begriff: "Uwe Böschemeyer", kurz: "German theologian and logotherapist, founder of value imagination.", text: "Uwe Böschemeyer studied theology and psychology and was trained in logotherapy by Viktor E. Frankl. He founded an institute for logotherapy and existential analysis in Hamburg and played a key role in the dissemination of logotherapy in northern Germany.\n\nWith value imagination he developed a combination of logotherapy and imagery work. He published numerous books on value-oriented personal development aimed at both professionals and a broad readership." },
  { key: "Irvin D. Yalom", begriff: "Irvin D. Yalom", kurz: "American psychiatrist and psychotherapist (born 1931), leading representative of existential psychotherapy and group psychotherapy.", text: "Irvin David Yalom was born in Washington, D.C., on June 13, 1931, and taught as professor of psychiatry at Stanford University. His textbook *The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy* (first edition 1970) is regarded as a standard work on group therapy; in it he described the therapeutic factors of groups.\n\nIn *Existential Psychotherapy* (1980) he systematized existential psychotherapy on the basis of the four givens of death, freedom, isolation, and meaninglessness. He also became known to a broad public through psychotherapeutic case stories and novels, such as *Love's Executioner* (1989) and *When Nietzsche Wept* (1992)." },
  { key: "Rollo May", begriff: "Rollo May", kurz: "American psychologist and psychotherapist (1909–1994), pioneer of existential psychology in the United States.", text: "Rollo May was born in Ada, Ohio, on April 21, 1909, and died in Tiburon, California, on October 22, 1994. He received his doctorate in clinical psychology from Columbia University; his dissertation was published as *The Meaning of Anxiety* (1950).\n\nTogether with Ernest Angel and Henri F. Ellenberger, he edited the volume *Existence* in 1958, which made European Daseinsanalysis and existential psychiatry known in the English-speaking world. Alongside Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, May is regarded as an important figure of the humanistic movement, although he placed greater emphasis on the tragic aspects of human existence." },
  { key: "Ludwig Binswanger", begriff: "Ludwig Binswanger", kurz: "Swiss psychiatrist (1881–1966), founder of daseinsanalytic psychiatry.", text: "Ludwig Binswanger was born in Kreuzlingen on April 13, 1881, and died there on February 5, 1966. For decades he directed the Bellevue Sanatorium in Kreuzlingen, founded by his grandfather, and maintained a lifelong friendship with Sigmund Freud.\n\nUnder the influence of Husserl and above all Heidegger, he developed Daseinsanalysis as a phenomenological-anthropological psychiatry. His principal works include *Grundformen und Erkenntnis menschlichen Daseins* (Basic Forms and Knowledge of Human Existence, 1942) and case studies such as *Der Fall Ellen West* (The Case of Ellen West)." },
  { key: "Medard Boss", begriff: "Medard Boss", kurz: "Swiss psychiatrist and psychoanalyst (1903–1990), founder of daseinsanalytic psychotherapy.", text: "Medard Boss was born in St. Gallen on October 4, 1903, and died in Zollikon on December 21, 1990. He underwent psychoanalytic training and later turned to the philosophy of Martin Heidegger.\n\nFrom 1959 to 1969, Boss and Heidegger held the “Zollikon Seminars” for physicians and psychiatrists at Boss's home. On this basis he developed Daseinsanalysis into an independent psychotherapeutic practice that largely detached itself from psychoanalytic constructs." },
  { key: "Martin Heidegger", begriff: "Martin Heidegger", kurz: "German philosopher (1889–1976) whose analytic of Dasein decisively influenced Daseinsanalysis and existential psychotherapy.", text: "Martin Heidegger was born in Meßkirch on September 26, 1889, and died in Freiburg im Breisgau on May 26, 1976. In his principal work *Sein und Zeit* (Being and Time, 1927) he analyzed the basic structures of human existence (Dasein), such as being-in-the-world, care, anxiety, and being-toward-death.\n\nHeidegger became significant for psychotherapy primarily through Ludwig Binswanger and Medard Boss. His role during the National Socialist era, in particular his rectorship at the University of Freiburg in 1933/34, is the subject of ongoing critical discussion." },
  { key: "Karl Jaspers", begriff: "Karl Jaspers", kurz: "German psychiatrist and philosopher (1883–1969), founder of understanding (verstehende) psychopathology and an important existential philosopher.", text: "Karl Jaspers was born in Oldenburg on February 23, 1883, and died in Basel on February 26, 1969. His *Allgemeine Psychopathologie* (General Psychopathology, 1913) established the methodological distinction between explaining and understanding in psychiatry and had a lasting influence on descriptive psychopathology.\n\nAs a philosopher, Jaspers developed an existential philosophy in which concepts such as “boundary situations” (suffering, struggle, guilt, death) and “existential communication” are central. These ideas show a clear affinity to Frankl's tragic triad." },
  { key: "Max Scheler", begriff: "Max Scheler", kurz: "German philosopher (1874–1928), founder of material value ethics and an important forerunner of philosophical anthropology.", text: "Max Scheler was born in Munich on August 22, 1874, and died in Frankfurt am Main on May 19, 1928. In his principal work *Der Formalismus in der Ethik und die materiale Wertethik* (Formalism in Ethics and Non-Formal Ethics of Values) he developed a theory of objective values standing in a hierarchical order, which are apprehended through feeling.\n\nFrankl explicitly referred to Scheler; Scheler's philosophy of values and his concept of the person influenced the logotherapeutic theory of values and the conception of the human being as a spiritual person. With *Die Stellung des Menschen im Kosmos* (The Human Place in the Cosmos, 1928), Scheler is also regarded as a co-founder of philosophical anthropology." },
  { key: "Viktor von Weizsäcker", begriff: "Viktor von Weizsäcker", kurz: "German physician (1886–1957), co-founder of psychosomatic medicine and representative of an anthropological medicine.", text: "Viktor von Weizsäcker was born in Stuttgart on April 21, 1886, and died in Heidelberg on January 8, 1957. In his work *Der Gestaltkreis* (1940) he described the inseparable unity of perception and movement and criticized a purely natural-scientific understanding of the organism.\n\nWeizsäcker called for introducing the subject into medicine and understood illness as a biographical event. His anthropological medicine is an important root of German-language psychosomatics. His role during the Nazi era is being critically examined in medical-historical research." },
  { key: "Sigmund Freud", begriff: "Sigmund Freud", kurz: "Austrian neurologist (1856–1939), founder of psychoanalysis.", text: "Sigmund Freud was born on May 6, 1856, in Freiberg, Moravia (today Příbor, Czech Republic), and died on September 23, 1939, in London, to which he had emigrated in 1938 to escape the National Socialists. He developed psychoanalysis as a theory of the unconscious, a research method, and a treatment procedure.\n\nFreud's central concepts are the unconscious, drive theory, the structural model of id, ego, and superego, defense mechanisms, and transference and countertransference. In the history of Viennese psychotherapy, psychoanalysis is regarded as the “First Viennese School”; Frankl opposed the pleasure principle with the will to meaning." },
  { key: "Alfred Adler", begriff: "Alfred Adler", kurz: "Austrian physician and psychotherapist (1870–1937), founder of individual psychology.", text: "Alfred Adler was born on February 7, 1870, in Rudolfsheim near Vienna and died on May 28, 1937, in Aberdeen, Scotland. He initially belonged to Freud's circle, broke with him in 1911, and founded individual psychology, which is often referred to as the “Second Viennese School” of psychotherapy.\n\nCentral concepts are the feeling of inferiority and its compensation, the striving for recognition and superiority, the style of life, and social interest (Gemeinschaftsgefühl). Adler was strongly engaged in child guidance and education. In his youth, Viktor E. Frankl belonged to his circle." },
  { key: "Carl R. Rogers", begriff: "Carl R. Rogers", kurz: "American psychologist (1902–1987), founder of client-centered and person-centered psychotherapy.", text: "Carl Ransom Rogers was born on January 8, 1902, in Oak Park, Illinois, and died on February 4, 1987, in La Jolla, California. From the 1940s onward he developed non-directive, later client-centered, and finally person-centered psychotherapy.\n\nIn 1957 Rogers formulated the “necessary and sufficient conditions” of therapeutic personality change, including empathy, congruence, and unconditional positive regard. He was one of the pioneers of empirical psychotherapy research, for instance through the audio recording of therapy sessions, and is regarded as one of the founders of humanistic psychology." },
  { key: "Aaron T. Beck", begriff: "Aaron T. Beck", kurz: "American psychiatrist (1921–2021), founder of cognitive therapy.", text: "Aaron Temkin Beck was born on July 18, 1921, in Providence, Rhode Island, and died on November 1, 2021, in Philadelphia. Originally trained in psychoanalysis, he developed cognitive therapy at the University of Pennsylvania in the 1960s, initially for depression.\n\nCentral concepts are automatic thoughts, cognitive distortions, dysfunctional core assumptions, and the cognitive triad of depression. Beck also developed widely used measurement instruments such as the Beck Depression Inventory. His work is among the foundations of today's cognitive behavioral therapy." },
  { key: "Albert Ellis", begriff: "Albert Ellis", kurz: "American psychologist (1913–2007), founder of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT).", text: "Albert Ellis was born on September 27, 1913, in Pittsburgh and died on July 24, 2007, in New York. After a psychoanalytic phase, he developed Rational Emotive Therapy from the mid-1950s onward, later called Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy.\n\nThe core of his approach is the ABC model: it is not the activating event (A) itself but the evaluations and beliefs (B) that determine the emotional and behavioral consequences (C). Irrational, especially absolutistic demands are questioned through disputation. Alongside Beck, Ellis is regarded as a pioneer of the cognitive turn in behavior therapy." },
  { key: "Steven C. Hayes", begriff: "Steven C. Hayes", kurz: "American psychologist (born 1948), co-founder of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy and Relational Frame Theory.", text: "Steven C. Hayes was born in 1948 and taught for many years at the University of Nevada, Reno. Together with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson, he developed Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT), whose goal is the promotion of psychological flexibility.\n\nIts theoretical foundation is Relational Frame Theory, which he co-founded, a behavior-analytic theory of language and cognition. ACT is counted among the so-called “third wave” approaches of behavior therapy; its emphasis on values-based action shows points of contact with meaning-centered approaches." },
  { key: "Marsha M. Linehan", begriff: "Marsha M. Linehan", kurz: "American psychologist (born 1943), founder of Dialectical Behavior Therapy.", text: "Marsha M. Linehan was born on May 5, 1943, in Tulsa, Oklahoma, and was a professor at the University of Washington in Seattle. She developed Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT), originally for chronically suicidal female patients with borderline personality disorder.\n\nDBT combines behavioral change strategies with acceptance and mindfulness elements from Zen practice. Central are the dialectical balance of acceptance and change as well as validation. In 2011 Linehan disclosed publicly that she herself had suffered from severe mental health problems in her youth." },
  { key: "Jeffrey E. Young", begriff: "Jeffrey E. Young", kurz: "American psychologist (born 1950), founder of schema therapy.", text: "Jeffrey E. Young initially worked with Aaron T. Beck at the Center for Cognitive Therapy in Philadelphia. Based on the experience that patients with chronic and personality-related problems benefited insufficiently from classical cognitive therapy, he developed schema therapy.\n\nSchema therapy integrates cognitive-behavioral, attachment-theoretical, psychodynamic, and experiential elements. Central concepts are early maladaptive schemas, coping styles, and schema modes. Young founded the Schema Therapy Institute in New York." },
  { key: "Paul Gilbert", begriff: "Paul Gilbert", kurz: "British clinical psychologist (born 1951), founder of Compassion Focused Therapy.", text: "Paul Gilbert developed Compassion Focused Therapy (CFT) particularly for people with pronounced shame and self-criticism. His approach is grounded in evolutionary psychology and describes three emotion regulation systems: threat and protection, drive and resource-seeking, and soothing and affiliation.\n\nGilbert founded the Compassionate Mind Foundation. CFT aims to systematically develop the capacity for compassion toward oneself and others and is now used transdiagnostically." },
  { key: "Martin E. P. Seligman", begriff: "Martin E. P. Seligman", kurz: "American psychologist (born 1942), known for the theory of learned helplessness and as the founder of positive psychology.", text: "Martin E. P. Seligman was born on August 12, 1942, in Albany, New York, and teaches at the University of Pennsylvania. In the 1960s and 1970s he investigated the phenomenon of learned helplessness, which became influential as a model of depressive disorders and was later reformulated in terms of attribution theory.\n\nAs president of the American Psychological Association, he proclaimed positive psychology as a research program in 1998. In his PERMA model of well-being, “Meaning” is one of five components, which creates points of contact with meaning research in the tradition of Frankl." },
  { key: "Psychologische Beratung", begriff: "Psychological Counseling", kurz: "Professional, time-limited support of individuals or groups in clarifying and coping with problems, decisions, and developmental tasks on a psychological basis.", text: "Psychological counseling is aimed at people seeking support with questions concerning life, relationships, parenting, career, or meaning, without necessarily having a mental disorder of clinical significance. It draws on psychological theories and methods, for example from the client-centered, systemic, cognitive-behavioral, or meaning-centered traditions.\n\nTypical fields of work are parenting and family counseling, couples and life counseling, academic and career counseling, workplace counseling, and crisis and grief support. A careful demarcation from psychotherapy is required professionally and – in Germany – also legally, especially when there are indications of mental illness." },
  { key: "Abgrenzung von Beratung und Psychotherapie", begriff: "Distinguishing Counseling from Psychotherapy", kurz: "Legal and professional distinction between counseling without reference to the practice of medicine (Heilkunde) and psychotherapy as the practice of medicine.", text: "In Germany, the practice of medicine (Heilkunde) requires authorization. Under the Heilpraktikergesetz (HeilprG, German Non-Medical Practitioners Act) of 1939, the practice of medicine is any activity carried out professionally or commercially to diagnose, cure, or alleviate diseases, ailments, or physical injuries in human beings. Anyone who practices medicine requires an Approbation (state license, for example as a physician or psychotherapist) or a permit under the HeilprG; such a permit can be granted restricted to the field of psychotherapy.\n\nThe Psychotherapeutengesetz (PsychThG, German Psychotherapists Act) defines the practice of psychotherapy as a medical activity to diagnose, cure, or alleviate disorders of clinical significance for which psychotherapy is indicated. Expressly excluded are activities whose subject is the processing or overcoming of social conflicts or other purposes outside the practice of medicine. The professional title “Psychotherapeutin” or “Psychotherapeut” (psychotherapist) is protected by law.\n\nPsychological counseling, coaching, and life counseling therefore do not require authorization, as long as they are aimed at people without a mental disorder requiring treatment and do not seek to diagnose or treat illnesses. In practice, this requires careful clarification of the client's concern, transparency toward those seeking advice, and, in the event of signs of mental illness, a recommendation to seek assessment by a medical specialist or psychotherapist. Different regulations apply in Austria and Switzerland." },
  { key: "Klientenzentrierte Gesprächsführung", begriff: "Client-Centered Counseling", kurz: "Form of conducting conversations going back to Carl R. Rogers, based on empathy, congruence, and unconditional positive regard.", text: "Client-centered counseling is based on the assumption that every human being has an actualizing tendency, that is, the potential for growth and self-development. The task of the counselor is not to prescribe solutions but to create a relational climate in which clients can understand themselves better and find their own paths.\n\nIn German-speaking countries, the approach was disseminated above all by Reinhard Tausch and Anne-Marie Tausch. The client-centered stance is today a foundation of almost all counseling approaches; as a psychotherapeutic method it is established as client-centered psychotherapy (Gesprächspsychotherapie)." },
  { key: "Empathie", begriff: "Empathy", kurz: "The capacity to understand another person's inner world as if it were one's own, without losing the “as if” quality.", text: "In Carl R. Rogers' sense, empathy (empathic understanding) means grasping the experience of another person as accurately as possible and communicating this understanding. What is decisive is that the other person feels understood.\n\nResearch distinguishes between cognitive empathy (perspective-taking) and affective empathy (emotional resonance). The therapist's empathy is among the best-supported relationship-related common factors in psychotherapy." },
  { key: "Kongruenz", begriff: "Congruence", kurz: "Genuineness of the counselor: correspondence between inner experience, awareness, and expression.", text: "For Rogers, congruence means that the counselor is “genuine” in the relationship and does not maintain a professional facade. He or she perceives his or her own experience and can, where helpful, also communicate it appropriately.\n\nCongruence does not mean expressing every one of one's own impulses unreflectively. Rather, it is a precondition for empathy and positive regard to come across as credible. Rogers regarded it as the most fundamental of the three attitudinal conditions." },
  { key: "Bedingungslose Wertschätzung", begriff: "Unconditional Positive Regard", kurz: "Acceptance of the client as a person without conditions or judgments.", text: "Unconditional positive regard denotes a warm, accepting attitude toward the whole person, regardless of which feelings, thoughts, or behaviors he or she expresses. It does not mean approving of every behavior but not making the person's worth dependent on conditions.\n\nAccording to Rogers, this attitude enables clients to meet themselves with less defensiveness and to integrate rejected experiences into their self-concept." },
  { key: "Aktives Zuhören", begriff: "Active Listening", kurz: "Conversational technique in which the listener attentively takes in and reflects back the content and emotional meaning of what is said.", text: "Active listening comprises nonverbal attentiveness, paraphrasing (summarizing content), verbalizing emotional experiences, and asking clarifying questions. The aim is to ensure understanding and to enable the other person to understand himself or herself more clearly.\n\nThe term was coined by Carl R. Rogers and Richard Farson and later popularized by Thomas Gordon, among others, for parenting and communication training. Active listening is a basic technique of almost all counseling and therapy approaches." },
  { key: "Motivational Interviewing", begriff: "Motivational Interviewing", kurz: "Client-centered yet goal-oriented style of conversation developed by William R. Miller and Stephen Rollnick to strengthen motivation for change.", text: "Motivational Interviewing (MI) was originally developed in the field of problematic alcohol use. Ambivalence about change is understood as normal; the aim is to elicit and strengthen the person's own reasons for change (“change talk”).\n\nThe underlying spirit comprises partnership, acceptance, compassion, and evocation. Core techniques include open questions, affirmations, reflective listening, and summaries. MI has been examined in numerous studies and meta-analyses and is now widely used in health care, addiction services, and counseling." },
  { key: "Ressourcenorientierung", begriff: "Resource Orientation", kurz: "Basic stance in counseling and therapy that deliberately takes up and makes use of a person's strengths, abilities, and supportive life conditions.", text: "Resources can be personal (for example abilities, values, humor), social (relationships, support), or material. Resource orientation means systematically exploring these and making them usable for coping with problems instead of focusing exclusively on deficits.\n\nKlaus Grawe described resource activation as a general common factor of psychotherapy. Resource orientation is particularly central in systemic, solution-focused, and meaning-centered approaches." },
  { key: "Lösungsorientierte Kurzberatung", begriff: "Solution-Focused Brief Counseling", kurz: "Approach developed by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg at the Brief Family Therapy Center in Milwaukee that focuses on solutions instead of problem analysis.", text: "The solution-focused approach (Solution-Focused Brief Therapy) assumes that solutions do not necessarily have to be derived from a precise analysis of the problem. Instead, exceptions to the problem, already existing abilities, and desired future states are explored.\n\nTypical interventions are the miracle question, scaling questions, exception questions, and compliments. The approach is usually designed to be brief and is used in counseling, social work, schools, and psychotherapy." },
  { key: "Wunderfrage", begriff: "Miracle Question", kurz: "Solution-focused intervention question that invites clients to imagine a state in which their problem has been solved overnight.", text: "The miracle question was developed in the circle of Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg. In essence, the question is: Suppose that while you are sleeping tonight a miracle happens and the problem is solved – how would you notice tomorrow morning that it has happened?\n\nThe question aims at describing concrete, observable features of a desirable future. These descriptions serve as orientation for goals and first small steps." },
  { key: "Skalierungsfrage", begriff: "Scaling Question", kurz: "Questioning technique in which clients indicate their assessment of a state on a scale, usually from 0 to 10.", text: "Scaling questions make subjective assessments such as confidence, distress, motivation, or progress open to discussion. Typical follow-up questions ask, for example, what already contributes to someone not being lower on the scale, or how he or she would recognize being one point higher.\n\nScaling questions are a core element of solution-focused brief counseling but are also used in systemic and cognitive-behavioral approaches and in Motivational Interviewing." },
  { key: "Zirkuläres Fragen", begriff: "Circular Questioning", kurz: "Systemic questioning technique in which a person is asked about the presumed thoughts, feelings, or relationships of other people.", text: "Circular questioning was described by the Milan group around Mara Selvini Palazzoli, Luigi Boscolo, Gianfranco Cecchin, and Giuliana Prata as part of their approach. A typical question would be: What do you think your daughter thinks when your husband withdraws?\n\nThe technique makes relationship patterns, differences, and mutual attributions visible and stimulates new perspectives. It is based on the systemic understanding that problems are maintained in circular interactions rather than in linear cause-and-effect chains." },
  { key: "Reframing", begriff: "Reframing", kurz: "Reinterpretation: a behavior or situation is placed in a new frame of meaning, which can change how it is evaluated.", text: "The concept was described in particular by Paul Watzlawick, John Weakland, and Richard Fisch at the Mental Research Institute in Palo Alto. In reframing, the facts remain unchanged, but their context of meaning is reconceived – for example, when “stubbornness” is understood as “perseverance” or a symptom as an attempted solution.\n\nReframing is a basic technique of systemic and solution-focused counseling. Related processes are found in cognitive restructuring and in the logotherapeutic modification of attitudes. A successful reframing must be plausible to the client and must not trivialize suffering." },
  { key: "Validierung", begriff: "Validation", kurz: "Communicative confirmation that a person's feelings, thoughts, or behaviors are comprehensible and understandable in the given context.", text: "Validation is a central element of Dialectical Behavior Therapy according to Marsha M. Linehan, who distinguishes several levels of validation – from attentive listening and articulating unverbalized experiences to radical genuineness.\n\nValidating does not mean approving of every behavior but acknowledging that it is understandable. Validation is considered a precondition for change strategies to be accepted and is now used in many counseling and therapy approaches. It is to be distinguished from validation according to Naomi Feil in the care of people with dementia." },
  { key: "Therapeutische Allianz", begriff: "Therapeutic Alliance", kurz: "Cooperative working relationship between client and counselor or therapist; also called the working alliance.", text: "In 1979 Edward S. Bordin described the alliance in a pantheoretical way by means of three components: agreement on goals, agreement on tasks or procedures, and a sustainable emotional bond. The term has psychoanalytic roots, for example in Ralph Greenson's “working alliance.”\n\nThe quality of the therapeutic alliance is one of the most frequently studied predictors of therapy success; meta-analyses show a consistent, moderate association with treatment outcome. Breaches in the alliance (“ruptures”) and their repair are regarded as significant process moments." },
  { key: "Übertragung", begriff: "Transference", kurz: "Psychoanalytic term for the displacement of feelings, wishes, and expectations from earlier relationships onto the person of the therapist.", text: "Sigmund Freud initially discovered transference as an obstacle to treatment and later recognized it as a central instrument of psychoanalytic therapy. In transference, early relationship patterns are revived in the current relationship and thus become accessible to therapeutic work.\n\nOutside psychoanalysis, too, it is recognized that transference phenomena can occur in any helping relationship. In counseling it is important to recognize them, even if they are not systematically worked through." },
  { key: "Gegenübertragung", begriff: "Countertransference", kurz: "The totality of the therapist's emotional reactions to the client, especially to the client's transference.", text: "Freud initially understood countertransference as a disruptive factor to be overcome through self-analysis. From 1950 onward, Paula Heimann and other authors broadened this understanding: countertransference was now also regarded as a valuable diagnostic instrument that can provide clues to the client's experience.\n\nReflective awareness of one's own reactions is important in all counseling and therapeutic professions. Supervision and peer consultation (intervision) are important settings for working through them." },
  { key: "Setting", begriff: "Setting", kurz: "The external framework of counseling or therapy, such as location, duration, frequency, participants, and mode of communication.", text: "The setting includes, among other things, the choice of individual, couple, family, or group format, the length and frequency of sessions, the physical environment, and the decision between in-person, telephone, or video counseling. Arrangements regarding fees, cancellations, and availability are also part of it.\n\nA clear, reliable setting provides security and orientation and is a precondition for a sustainable working relationship. In psychodynamic approaches, it is also understood as a significant component of the therapeutic situation itself." },
  { key: "Kontrakt", begriff: "Contract", kurz: "Explicit agreement between client and counselor on the concerns, goals, procedure, and framework of the collaboration.", text: "The contract clarifies what is to be worked on, what can realistically be achieved, and what responsibilities both sides bear. It also includes agreements on setting, confidentiality, fees, and termination. When several parties are involved, for instance in organizations, a triangular contract between the commissioning party, the client, and the counselor is often required.\n\nA careful contract serves transparency and the demarcation between counseling and the practice of medicine (Heilkunde). It is reviewed over the course of the work and adjusted as needed." },
  { key: "Psychoedukation", begriff: "Psychoeducation", kurz: "Systematic, didactically prepared conveying of knowledge about psychological stress or disorders and their treatment to affected persons and their relatives.", text: "Psychoeducation is intended to promote understanding of symptoms, causes, course, and treatment options, to reduce stigmatization and feelings of guilt, and to strengthen self-efficacy in dealing with the illness. It is offered in individual or group formats and is frequently a component of cognitive-behavioral treatments.\n\nPsychoeducational groups for relatives have proven effective particularly in schizophrenic and affective disorders. In counseling as well, for example on stress or burnout, conveying explanatory models is an important element." },
  { key: "Krisenintervention", begriff: "Crisis Intervention", kurz: "Short-term, goal-directed support for people in acute psychosocial crises with the aim of stabilization and restoration of the capacity to act.", text: "The foundations of crisis theory go back, among others, to Gerald Caplan; in German-speaking countries, Johan Cullberg's distinction between traumatic crises and crises of change is also widespread. Crises arise when an event exceeds a person's available coping resources.\n\nCrisis intervention is prompt, time-limited, and structured. It comprises establishing contact, assessing risk – especially suicidality –, emotional relief, activating resources and social support, and, where appropriate, referral to further help. In cases of acute danger to self or others, medical or emergency medical assessment is required." },
  { key: "Supervision", begriff: "Supervision", kurz: "Professional reflection on one's professional practice under the guidance of a qualified supervisor.", text: "Supervision serves quality assurance, the further development of professional competencies, and the protection of clients as well as professionals. It takes place as individual, group, or team supervision and can be oriented toward cases, teams, or organizations.\n\nIn psychotherapeutic training and continuing education, supervision is a mandatory component. In counseling as well, regular supervision is considered a professional standard, particularly for working through difficult case trajectories and countertransference reactions and for preventing overload." },
  { key: "Intervision", begriff: "Intervision", kurz: "Peer consultation among professionals of equal standing without external leadership.", text: "In intervision, colleagues jointly reflect on cases or professional questions. It frequently follows a fixed sequence with assigned roles, such as case presenter, moderator, and consultants, and phases of case presentation, hypothesis formation, solution ideas, and conclusion.\n\nIntervision complements supervision but does not completely replace it. It promotes diversity of perspectives, mutual support, and professional self-reflection." },
  { key: "Burnout-Beratung", begriff: "Burnout Counseling", kurz: "Counseling for people with work-related exhaustion aimed at clarifying stress factors, providing relief, and prevention.", text: "The term burnout was coined in the 1970s by Herbert J. Freudenberger and elaborated empirically by Christina Maslach; Maslach described the dimensions of emotional exhaustion, depersonalization or cynicism, and reduced personal accomplishment. In ICD-11, burnout is listed as an occupational phenomenon in the chapter “Factors influencing health status” and is not classified as a mental disorder.\n\nBurnout counseling comprises psychoeducation, analysis of individual and organizational stress factors, strengthening recovery and boundary-setting, and clarifying the values and sources of meaning in one's work. Because of the overlap with depressive illnesses, careful assessment and, where appropriate, referral to medical or psychotherapeutic treatment are important." },
  { key: "Trauerbegleitung", begriff: "Grief Counseling", kurz: "Support for people who have suffered a significant loss, especially through the death of a close person.", text: "Grief is a normal reaction to loss and not a sign of illness. More recent models such as the Dual Process Model of Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut describe grief as an oscillation between loss-oriented and restoration-oriented coping; J. William Worden formulated “tasks of mourning,” and Verena Kast developed a phase model in the German-speaking world.\n\nGrief counseling offers space for feelings and memories and supports adjustment to changed life circumstances and the search for a continuing bond with the deceased. If grief is persistent and severely impairing, prolonged grief disorder may be present, which is listed as a separate diagnosis in ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR and requires psychotherapeutic treatment." },
  { key: "Schweigepflicht", begriff: "Duty of Confidentiality", kurz: "Legal and professional-ethical obligation not to disclose information entrusted by clients to third parties without authorization.", text: "In Germany, § 203 of the Strafgesetzbuch (StGB, German Criminal Code) makes the unauthorized disclosure of others' secrets by certain professional groups a criminal offense. These include, among others, physicians, professional psychologists with a state-recognized academic final examination, psychotherapists, and counselors in recognized counseling centers. In addition, data protection regulations apply, in particular the GDPR (DSGVO).\n\nDisclosure may be permissible with the consent of the person concerned or in the case of a justifying emergency (§ 34 StGB), for example in the event of acute danger to life and limb. Irrespective of its scope under criminal law, confidentiality is one of the fundamental professional-ethical standards of all counseling and should be transparently regulated in the contract." }
]);
